{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q008_v1_atp_synthase_bulge_classification","answer":"Verified in SCOPe 2.08 (the current stable release): the bulge domain is superfamily b.84.5 (sunid 310577), "V1 ATP synthase A subunit, bulge domain-like". It has one family, b.84.5.1 (sunid 310616), with two protein nodes: V1 ATP synthase A subunit bulge domain (310706) and A1 ATP synthase A subunit bulge domain (310707). Its lineage is class b (All beta proteins, 48724), then fold b.84 (Barrel-sandwich hybrid, 51229), then the superfamily. The fold has five superfamilies: b.84.1 single hybrid motif (51230), b.84.2 rudiment single hybrid motif (51246), b.84.3 duplicated hybrid motif (51261), b.84.4 ribosomal L27 protein-like (110324) and b.84.5. The bulge domain is therefore its own superfamily inside the shared fold, and not a family of any of the other four.

Interpretation: a separate superfamily within one fold is SCOPe's way of recording two things. The domain has a similar overall topology (a barrel-sandwich hybrid), so it sits in the same fold. But SCOPe does not assert common ancestry with the b.84.1–b.84.4 superfamilies at the superfamily level. The usual SCOP criterion is that a superfamily groups domains with enough structural, functional or sequence evidence to infer probable common ancestry. Here the bulge domain has no such evidence linking it to the other hybrid-motif groups. The 2.08 annotation for b.84.5 cites a literature reference, PubMed 19893485. I did not retrieve or read that paper, so I can't verify the specific structural or sequence argument behind creating the new superfamily.

Evolutionary claim made: the placement claims only that the V1 (and A1) A-subunit bulge-domain family members are probably homologous to each other (a shared superfamily, and a shared family b.84.5.1 for the two proteins). At the fold level it records structural similarity to the other barrel-sandwich-hybrid groups, and nothing stronger. It is not a claim that the bulge domain is unrelated, analogous, convergent or independently evolved relative to the other b.84 superfamilies. Separate superfamilies mean only that SCOPe makes no positive homology assertion between them. A distant relationship is not excluded, but it is not supported by SCOPe evidence either. Nothing in the hierarchy or annotations tells me the domain's function or mechanism in ATP synthase. Fold membership gives no basis for transferring function or using another b.84 domain as a template. The homology endpoint for 310577 returned no warnings, heterogeneity or repeat flags. That absence is not independent proof of homology. I also did not query earlier releases, so I make no claim about when or why this superfamily was introduced.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ATP%20synthase%20A%20subunit%20bulge&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51229","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51229/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310616/children?limit=20"]}]}
